    "content": "Uganda has confirmed that its military forces will maintain their deployment in Somalia, according to a statement made by Defense Minister Kiryowa Kiwanuka. The announcement underscores Kampala's ongoing participation in the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS), which supports Somali security forces in their fight against militant groups.\n\nThe minister's remarks come amid a broader security transition in Somalia, where ATMIS is gradually handing over responsibilities to Somali forces ahead of a scheduled withdrawal. Uganda, a key troop-contributing country, has been involved in peacekeeping operations in Somalia since 2007, providing thousands of troops to help stabilize the country.\n\nThe decision to extend the stay reflects Uganda's commitment to preventing a security vacuum that could undermine gains made over the past decade. It also aligns with requests from Somali authorities and international partners for continued support during the current drawdown phase.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development carries significant weight. Many diaspora members have family and business interests in both Somalia and Uganda, and sustained peacekeeping contributes to safer conditions for travel, remittances, and future investments. Stability in Somalia also reduces the need for displacement and supports voluntary return.\n\nUganda's reaffirmation signals long-term regional cooperation on security, but challenges remain, including funding for peacekeeping missions and the readiness of Somali forces. The coming months will be critical as the transition progresses, with international observers watching whether security gains can be maintained.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/dowladda-uganda-oo-ku-dhawaaqday-in-ciidankeedu-sii-joogi-doonaan-somalia/424834/",
