    "content": "Egypt and Ethiopia are considering joining the Makkah Defense Agreement, a regional security pact recently signed by Saudi Arabia, Türkiye, and Pakistan. Egypt's Foreign Minister Badr Abdelatty said Cairo must first review legal and constitutional requirements before committing to such international defense arrangements.\n\nAbdelatty made the remarks during a press conference with Turkish Foreign Minister Hakan Fidan in New Alamein, Egypt. He noted that Egypt was previously informed about the Makkah pact and is part of a broader cooperation framework that includes the four countries, with relations among them continuing to develop.\n\nThe agreement, which aims to enhance collective security cooperation among Muslim-majority nations, has drawn interest from other states in the region. Ethiopia's potential involvement signals growing diplomatic engagement between Addis Ababa and the signatories, including Egypt, with whom it shares historical ties and unresolved water disputes.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the expansion of this pact could influence the Horn of Africa's security dynamics, particularly because Egypt, Ethiopia, and Türkiye have active ties with Somalia. The pact may create new alignments that affect stability and development projects in the region, directly impacting diaspora investments and community safety.\n\nWhile the pact's specific provisions remain undisclosed, its enlargement may offer a forum for dialogue on shared challenges like terrorism and maritime security. However, observers caution that its success depends on reconciling differing national interests, especially among states with competing agendas in the Horn.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/14/masar-iyo-itoobiya-oo-ku-biiraya-heshiiska-difaaca-makkah/",
